From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] aic8800_fdrv: keep draining the rx queue after a bad aggregate header

This board runs the aggregated prealloc rx path: the module enumerates as
0xa69c:0x8d81, which is USB_PRODUCT_ID_AIC8800D81, and aicwf_usb_chipmatch()
sets aicwf_usb_rx_aggr = true for it. In that path, aicwf_process_rxframes()
returns -EBADE when a header claims more than the buffer holds. It frees that
one buffer and leaves every buffer queued behind it in rxq.

The consumer then stops for good. usb_busrx_thread() sleeps on busrx_trgg, and
aicwf_usb_rx_complete() calls complete() only when rx_cnt goes from 0 to 1.
With k buffers left in the queue, rx_cnt stays at k or higher, so every later
enqueue misses that edge. Unless a wake-up is already pending, nothing runs the
queue again. Completing URBs keep taking buffers from the prealloc pool and
parking them in rxq until the pool is empty. Then aicwf_usb_submit_rx_urb()
fails for all 20 URBs ("failed to alloc rxbuff", "sub rx fail"). Only the
consumer re-arms them (rx_urb_sched, then rx_urb_work), and it is asleep.

Both logged runs that carry the parser line end that way (2026-09-11 and 2026-09-12):
- 2026-09-11 soak, run 1 (4.0 pin, same parser): `pkt_len:27704
  buffer->len:974` at 523 s, pool empty and 20 x `sub rx fail` at 706 s, rx
  0.22 Mbit/s over 724 s.
- 2026-09-12 USB-path run 1 `pkt_len:42382 buffer->len:5780` at
  418 s, traffic stopped at 440 s, pool 980 -> 435 -> 257 -> 205 while EHCI
  kept completing transfers.
The 900 s run of 2026-09-12 that did not fail has no parser line. This is a
code reading plus two logs, not a silicon proof.

What changes, in the aggregated prealloc branch only:
- A too-long header drops the rest of that aggregate, and the loop goes on with
  the next queued buffer. The normal end-of-buffer path frees the buffer,
  honours rx_urb_sched and decrements rx_cnt.
- The two allocation failures in the same loop do the same. The skb one
  returned like the length check. The kmalloc one also returned without
  atomic_dec(), which left rx_cnt one high for good, so the 0 -> 1 edge could
  never fire again.

Diagnostics under /sys/module/aic8800_fdrv/parameters (read-only unless noted):
- rx_bad_len_drops: aggregates dropped by the length check.
- rx_bad_len_backlog_max: the most buffers queued behind a dropped aggregate
  (rx_cnt at the drop, minus the dropped buffer). At 1 or more, the vendor
  return would have left those buffers behind a consumer that is not woken.
- rx_inject_bad_len (0644): test stimulus. The next N aggregate headers count
  as too long. 0 by default.
- rx_urb_errors, rx_urb_last_status: bulk-IN URBs that completed with a status
  below 0. Such a URB goes back to the free list, and the next good completion
  of another URB re-arms it through aicwf_usb_rx_submit_all_urb(). If every URB
  in flight fails, nothing re-arms them; rx_urbs_in_flight 0 with rx_urb_errors
  above 0 shows that case. Counted because the second failure image
  (`Txmsg wait timed out` with no parser line) is unexplained.
- rx_urbs_in_flight: rx_urb_cnt, URBs submitted and not yet completed.
- rx_queued_at_enqueue: rx_cnt right after the last enqueue.

Left as the vendor has it:
- The non-aggregated prealloc branch and the skb branches. This board does not
  take or build them, so nothing here can prove a change to them. The
  non-aggregated prealloc branch has the same missing atomic_dec() on a kmalloc
  failure.
- The 0 -> 1 wake-up edge itself. It is correct while rx_cnt counts every
  queued buffer, and the paths above now keep that true.
- down(&aicwf_deinit_sem) in URB completion context on a status below 0: a
  sleeping call in atomic context, a separate defect.
- rx_urb_sched is a plain bool: set by a failed submit after the consumer's
  last check, it re-arms nothing until the consumer frees the next buffer.
- The length check does not cover the copies after it (pkt_len + 60 bytes for
  data, aggr_len + 4 for config), so both can read a few bytes past the buffer.
- aic_load_fw's own parser (skb_pull return ignored): a separate change.

Falsifier, on the board under WLAN rx load: write N to rx_inject_bad_len.
Expected: rx_bad_len_drops rises by N, rx traffic continues, and aic_load_fw's
rx_buff_list_ava returns to its idle value. A stall, a draining pool or
`sub rx fail` refutes the change.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_txrxif.c
+++ b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_txrxif.c
@@ -463,6 +463,9 @@
 #endif
 
 extern bool rx_urb_sched;
+extern uint rx_bad_len_drops;
+extern int rx_inject_bad_len;
+extern int rx_bad_len_backlog_max;
 int aicwf_process_rxframes(struct aicwf_rx_priv *rx_priv)
 {
 #ifdef AICWF_SDIO_SUPPORT
@@ -585,11 +588,18 @@
                 pkt_len = (*data | (*(data + 1) << 8));
                 //printk("%s cnt:%d pkt_len:%d \r\n", __func__, cnt, pkt_len);
 #ifndef CONFIG_USB_RX_REASSEMBLE
+                if (unlikely(rx_inject_bad_len > 0)) {
+                    rx_inject_bad_len--;
+                    pkt_len = buffer->len + 1;
+                }
                 if (pkt_len > buffer->len) {
                     AICWFDBG(LOGERROR, "%s pkt_len:%d buffer->len:%d\r\n", __func__, pkt_len, buffer->len);
-                    aicwf_prealloc_rxbuff_free(buffer, &rx_priv->rxbuff_lock);
-                    atomic_dec(&rx_priv->rx_cnt);
-                    return -EBADE;
+                    /* Drop the rest of this aggregate, keep draining the queue. */
+                    rx_bad_len_drops++;
+                    /* rx_cnt still counts this buffer: the rest were queued behind it */
+                    if (atomic_read(&rx_priv->rx_cnt) - 1 > rx_bad_len_backlog_max)
+                        rx_bad_len_backlog_max = atomic_read(&rx_priv->rx_cnt) - 1;
+                    break;
                 }
 #endif
 
@@ -599,9 +609,7 @@
                     skb_inblock = __dev_alloc_skb(aggr_len + CCMP_OR_WEP_INFO, GFP_KERNEL);
                     if (skb_inblock == NULL) {
                         txrx_err("no more space! skip\n");
-                        aicwf_prealloc_rxbuff_free(buffer, &rx_priv->rxbuff_lock);
-                        atomic_dec(&rx_priv->rx_cnt);
-                        return -EBADE;
+                        break;
                     }
 
                     skb_put(skb_inblock, aggr_len);
@@ -620,8 +628,7 @@
                     msg = kmalloc(aggr_len+4, GFP_KERNEL);
                     if(msg == NULL){
                         txrx_err("no more space for msg!\n");
-                        aicwf_prealloc_rxbuff_free(buffer, &rx_priv->rxbuff_lock);
-                        return -EBADE;
+                        break;
                     }
                     memcpy(msg, data, aggr_len + 4);
 
--- a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_usb.c
+++ b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_usb.c
@@ -56,6 +56,44 @@
 #endif
 atomic_t rx_urb_cnt;
 bool rx_urb_sched = false;
+
+/* rx diagnostics, read under /sys/module/aic8800_fdrv/parameters */
+static uint rx_urb_errors;
+module_param(rx_urb_errors, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_urb_errors, "Bulk-IN rx URBs that completed with an error");
+static int rx_urb_last_status;
+module_param(rx_urb_last_status, int, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_urb_last_status, "urb->status of the last failed bulk-IN rx URB");
+uint rx_bad_len_drops;
+module_param(rx_bad_len_drops, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_bad_len_drops, "rx aggregates dropped because a header claimed more than the buffer holds");
+int rx_inject_bad_len;
+module_param(rx_inject_bad_len, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_inject_bad_len, "Test stimulus: treat the next N rx aggregate headers as too long");
+int rx_bad_len_backlog_max;
+module_param(rx_bad_len_backlog_max, int, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_bad_len_backlog_max, "Most buffers queued behind a dropped aggregate (the vendor code left them unprocessed)");
+static int rx_queued_at_enqueue;
+module_param(rx_queued_at_enqueue, int, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_queued_at_enqueue, "rx_cnt right after the last enqueue: buffers waiting for the busrx thread");
+
+static int rx_urbs_in_flight_get(char *buf, const struct kernel_param *kp)
+{
+    return sysfs_emit(buf, "%d\n", atomic_read(&rx_urb_cnt));
+}
+
+/* parse_one() calls ->set unchecked for a modprobe argument */
+static int rx_urbs_in_flight_set(const char *val, const struct kernel_param *kp)
+{
+    return -EPERM;
+}
+
+static const struct kernel_param_ops rx_urbs_in_flight_ops = {
+    .set = rx_urbs_in_flight_set,
+    .get = rx_urbs_in_flight_get,
+};
+module_param_cb(rx_urbs_in_flight, &rx_urbs_in_flight_ops, NULL, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_urbs_in_flight, "Bulk-IN rx URBs submitted and not yet completed");
 u32 aicwf_usb_max_pkt_size = AICWF_USB_MAX_PKT_SIZE;
 
 void aicwf_usb_tx_flowctrl(struct rwnx_hw *rwnx_hw, bool state)
@@ -297,6 +335,8 @@
         aicwf_usb_rx_buf_put(usb_dev, usb_buf);
         if(urb->status < 0){
             AICWFDBG(LOGDEBUG, "%s urb->status:%d \r\n", __func__, urb->status);
+            rx_urb_errors++;
+            rx_urb_last_status = urb->status;
 
             if(g_rwnx_plat->wait_disconnect_cb == false){
                 g_rwnx_plat->wait_disconnect_cb = true;
@@ -334,6 +374,7 @@
         }
         spin_unlock_irqrestore(&rx_priv->rxqlock, flags);
         atomic_inc(&rx_priv->rx_cnt);
+        rx_queued_at_enqueue = atomic_read(&rx_priv->rx_cnt);
 
         if(atomic_read(&rx_priv->rx_cnt) == 1){
             complete(&rx_priv->usbdev->bus_if->busrx_trgg);
